course: dictionary
lesson: 4643
title: Hiss: a sound and a warning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A hiss is a long «sss» sound, like a snake, steam or air escaping. You can hiss (the action) or make a hiss (the sound). Context tells you which meaning the author wants.
explain: «A hiss is a cat's warning to stay away.» The sentence tells you the hiss is a warning, so it means the cat is saying, in its own way, "Keep back!"
explain: In stories, hissing usually means danger or anger. «The grass snake defends itself by hissing» shows the hiss is a way of protecting itself. Words like «hissed through her teeth» show anger, as a sharp, angry whisper.
explain: Hiss is also an onomatopoeia, a word that sounds like its meaning. Read it aloud and you can hear the sound it describes. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
fact: Hiss can be a verb (to hiss) or a noun (a hiss, hisses).
fact: To hiss is to make a long «sss» sound, like a snake or steam escaping.
fact: «A hiss is a cat's warning to stay away.»
fact: To hiss can mean to say something in a sharp, angry whisper, as in «hissed through her teeth».
fact: An audience may hiss to show they dislike someone. Hiss is an onomatopoeia: it sounds like its meaning.
exercise: «A hiss is a cat's warning to stay away.» What does the hiss mean here? | A sign the cat wants a cuddle | *A warning to stay away | A way of asking for food | A loud crash
exercise: The kettle began to hiss. What is making the sound? | A dog barking | A bird singing | *Steam escaping | A door slamming
exercise: «The grass snake defends itself by hissing.» What does defends mean? | Swims away | Eats frogs | Hides under a rock | *Protects itself
test: «The Australian frilled lizard opens a huge neck frill and hisses to startle predators.» Why does the lizard hiss? | To call its friends | *To startle predators | To go to sleep | To sing a song
test: «She hissed through her teeth.» What does this show? | She was steaming hot | She was singing softly | She was laughing loudly | *She spoke in a sharp, angry whisper
exam: «A skunk ... warns first by stamping, hissing and raising its tail.» What does hissing mean here? | To fly away | To fall asleep | *To make a long «sss» sound | To jump high
